{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.app.tsbuildinfo",
    "target": "ES2023",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "types": ["vite/client", "vitest/globals"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    /* mobile-client/ is the phone's client: real .js files that src-tauri's
       build script glues into one page. `allowJs` is what puts them into the
       program at all — without it the tests below could not import them and
       the whole extraction would be cosmetic. `checkJs` stays off: the client
       is ES5-style browser code and turning it on would demand JSDoc types
       inside files whose bytes are the shipped page (measured 01.09.2026:
       61 errors, all of them DOM narrowing).

       What tsc DOES enforce, and it is checked by a probe: an export the
       TypeScript tests import has to exist. Take PERSONAS off personas.js's
       list and tsc says TS2459 in two test files. The names only client.js
       imports are outside that — a .js importer with checkJs off is not
       checked — and eslint's `no-undef` covers those instead. */
    "allowJs": true,

    /* Bundler mode */
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    /* Linting */
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "erasableSyntaxOnly": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  },
  "include": ["src", "mobile-client"]
}
